### 3. Functional Interpretations of Core Interactors

Clicking on the individual network nodes highlights why these specific proteins interact dynamically with the TP53 hub to coordinate DNA damage responses:

| Interacting Protein | Full Name / Description | Biological Reason for Interaction with TP53 |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **MDM2** | E3 Ubiquitin-Protein Ligase | Acts as the primary negative regulator ("off-switch"). It binds to TP53 in healthy cells, tagging it for ubiquitin-mediated degradation to prevent unnecessary cell arrest. |
| **ATM** | Serine/Threonine-Protein Kinase | Acts as the upstream "alarm system" for double-strand DNA breaks. It phosphorylates TP53, stabilizing it and blocking MDM2 from degrading it. |
| **CHEK2** | Checkpoint Kinase 2 | A helper kinase activated by DNA damage that works in tandem with ATM to phosphorylate and activate TP53 during replication stress. |
| **BRCA1** | Breast Cancer Type 1 Susceptibility | Coordinates complex DNA repair pathways and works alongside TP53 to arrest the cell cycle so genetic codes can be safely repaired. |

### 4. Biological Conclusion
This network exercise demonstrates that proteins do not operate in isolation. TP53 acts as a vital structural and functional communication traffic controller. Disrupting any single edge or node within this specialized pathway can cause the network to collapse, leading directly to uncontrolled cell proliferation and oncogenesis.
